            <ArticleTitle>Electrocardiographic Predictors of Morbidity and Mortality in Patients with Acute Myocarditis: the Importance of QRS-T Angle.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1071-9164(17)31275-7</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cardfail.2017.11.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Acute myocarditis carries a variable prognosis. We evaluated the morbidity and mortality rates in patients with acute myocarditis and admission electrocardiographic predictors of outcome.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patients admitted to a tertiary hospital with a clinical diagnosis of acute myocarditis were evaluated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">193 patients were included. Median follow-up was 5.7 years; 82% were males, median age of 30 years (21-39). The most common clinical presentations were chest pain (77%) and fever (53%). The 30-day survival rate was 98.9%. Overall survival during follow-up was 94.3%. The most common abnormalities observed on electrocardiogram were T wave changes (36%) and ST segment changes (32%). Less frequent changes included abnormal T wave axis (&gt;105° or &lt; -15°, 16%), abnormal QRS axis (12%), QTc &gt; 460 (11%) and QRS interval ≥ 120 ms (5%). Wide QRS-T angle (≥100(o)) was demonstrated in 13% of the patients and was associated with an increased mortality rate compared to patients with a narrow QRS-T angle (20% vs. 4%; p=0.007). The rate of heart failure among patients with a wide QRS-T angle was significantly higher (36% vs. 10%; p=0.001). Cox regression analysis demonstrated that a wide QRS-T angle (≥100(o)) was a significant independent predictor of heart failure (HR 3.20, 95% CI 1.35-7.59, P&lt;0.01) and of the combined endpoint of death or heart failure (HR 2.56, 95% CI 1.14-5.75, P&lt;0.05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">QRS-T angle is a predictor of increased morbidity and mortality in acute myocarditis.</AbstractText>
